reply.body:
Both do same CA cert checks. Differences only apply to the first cert in the chain (i.e. the end entity cert).
- _Subject Alt Name_: Server hostname checking uses SAN dnsName & ipAddress. Client authN doesn't do anything. The caller is free to implement something else based on use case (ex: SubjectDN or SAN.directoryName for LDAP, SAN.otherName for Win DC legacy SAM account, SAN.rfc822Name for email address).
- _Key Usage_: Client authN cipher suites support RSA sign & EC sign. Server supports RSA sign, EC sign, RSA encrypt, and ECDH key agreement. Server has more flexibility.
- _Extended Key Usage_: Server supports null EKU or serverAuth. Client supports null EKU or clientAuth.

I learned by skimming through EndEntityChecker.check(). It is well documented and easy to understand. Drill down into checkTLSClient() and checkTLSServer(), and you can see the differences I mentioned for KU and EKU.
- https://github.com/AdoptOpenJDK/openjdk-jdk11/blob/master/src/java.base/share/classes/sun/security/validator/EndEntityChecker.java#L142

